In Apache Airflow, a DAG—or Directed Acyclic Graph—is the master blueprint of your data pipeline.
Written in Python, it defines exactly what tasks need to run, in what order, and when, 
acting as the traffic cop for your data infrastructure without actually processing any of the data itself.

Technical Breakdown :- 
Directed: The pipeline has a strict, one-way execution flow.
If you write Task_A >> Task_B, Airflow guarantees that Task B will not start until Task A has successfully completed.

Acyclic: There are no infinite loops allowed. Data flows from a starting point to a completion state. 
Task B cannot point back to Task A; if it did, the pipeline would trap itself in a cycle and never finish.

Graph: This is the visual topology.
The 'nodes' are your Operators (like a SnowflakeOperator executing a query or a BashOperator running a script),
and the 'edges' are the dependency rules connecting them.

Buisness Context :- 
Before Airflow, businesses relied on messy, disconnected 'cron' jobs scattered across random servers.
If a 2 AM extraction failed, the 3 AM transformation would still run on empty data, 
and executives would get blank reports at 8 AM. Airflow DAGs provide centralized observability;
if a task fails, the DAG pauses downstream tasks, alerts the engineering team, 
and allows for one-click retries, guaranteeing data SLAs.

Design Decision :- 
The most common mistake I see some engineers make is treating Airflow like an execution engine,
writing heavy Pandas data transformations directly inside a PythonOperator.
This bloats the Airflow worker nodes and causes OOM crashes. As a data engineer,
I strictly enforce the rule: Airflow is an orchestrator, not a bulldozer. 
I design DAGs using the KubernetesPodOperator or cloud-native triggers (like DatabricksSubmitRunOperator).
The DAG simply pings external, scalable compute clusters to do the heavy lifting, 
monitors their status, and moves to the next step.
